import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero

meta = {
	title: "HTTP: other servers, bodies, cookies",
	description: "The Roc Solid server platform and HTTP: requests to other servers with Http.fetch!, their limits, kept-alive connections and proxies, answers read as they arrive, request bodies as bytes and multipart forms, answers passed on from elsewhere, cookies and their attributes, and route files for .well-known paths and every method.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "out", label: "Asking another server" },
		{ id: "limits-out", label: "Limits and errors" },
		{ id: "connections", label: "Connections and proxies" },
		{ id: "chunks", label: "Reading an answer as it arrives" },
		{ id: "bytes", label: "Request bodies are bytes" },
		{ id: "forward", label: "Passing on a body from elsewhere" },
		{ id: "cookies", label: "Cookies" },
		{ id: "routes", label: ".well-known, and every method" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform · HTTP"
				title="Other servers, bodies as they came, and cookies"
			>
				The server answers HTTP, and it can ask too: an API's JSON, a token from a sign-in provider, a
				page someone links to. This page covers requests to other servers, how a request's body reaches
				your code, answers made of bytes or passed through from elsewhere, and cookies.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				Every sample on this page is from <code>server/test/app/Samples.roc</code>, which the server
				platform's tests compile and run.
			</p>

			<Heading id="out">Asking another server</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_forecast.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_forecast.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>{"Http.fetch!(url, init)"}</code> works like JavaScript's <code>fetch</code>: it sends a
				request and waits for the whole answer. The call pauses while it waits, holding no thread, so the
				rest of the server goes on (<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#calls"}>how calls pause</a>).
				<code>init</code> is an <code>Http.Init</code>, a record whose every field has a default, so
				<code>{"{}"}</code> is a plain GET. Its fields:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<code>method</code>: <code>Get</code>, <code>Post</code>, <code>Put</code>, <code>Patch</code>,
					<code>Delete</code>, <code>Head</code>, <code>Options</code>, or <code>{"Other(\"PROPFIND\")"}</code>
					for any other, sent as written.
				</li>
				<li><code>headers</code>: <code>{"(name, value)"}</code> pairs, sent as given.</li>
				<li>
					<code>query</code>: pairs, each percent-encoded and added to the URL's query (after an
					<code>&amp;</code> if the URL has a query already).
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>body</code>: <code>Empty</code> (the default), or <code>{"Http.json(value)"}</code>,
					<code>{"Http.form(pairs)"}</code>, <code>{"Http.plain_text(text)"}</code> or
					<code>{"Http.bytes(list)"}</code>. Each sets its <code>content-type</code> unless
					<code>headers</code> has one; bytes set none, as <code>fetch</code> does for a
					<code>Uint8Array</code>.
				</li>
				<li><code>timeout_ms</code>, <code>max_bytes</code> and <code>redirect</code>: the limits, below.</li>
				<li><code>proxy</code>: <code>FromEnv</code>, <code>Direct</code> or <code>{"Via(url)"}</code>, below.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				You can write a record literal wherever an <code>Http.Init</code> is expected. To keep one and build
				on it, annotate it, as <code>authed</code> is above, so a record update can add to it:
				<code>{"{ ..authed, method: Delete }"}</code>.
			</p>
			<p>
				Any status is an answer: <code>Ok</code> holds an <code>HttpResponse</code> with
				<code>res.status</code>, <code>res.ok</code> (true for a 2xx), <code>res.headers</code>,
				<code>{"res.header(name)"}</code> (any case), <code>{"res.text()"}</code>,
				<code>{"res.json()"}</code> (decoded as the type you ask for, or <code>InvalidJson(why)</code>) and
				<code>{"res.bytes()"}</code>. <code>Err</code> means there was no answer at all.
			</p>
			<p>
				An older API (<code>Http.get</code>, <code>post_form</code>, <code>post_json</code>,
				<code>with_header</code>, <code>{"send!(request, opts)"}</code>, <code>send_request!</code>,
				<code>Http.header</code>, <code>Http.text</code>) still works but is deprecated: each is
				<code>fetch!</code> underneath. The <a href={Paths.ecosystem_storage}>storage package</a> is given
				<code>Http.send!</code> because it names no platform, so it can't name <code>Http.Init</code>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits-out">Limits and errors</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_preview.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_preview.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				By default a request may take 30 seconds (<code>timeout_ms</code>), its answer's body may be up to
				64 MiB (<code>max_bytes</code>), and redirects are followed (<code>redirect: Follow</code>). For a
				URL a stranger gave you, ask for less, as above. <code>0</code> means no limit, and
				<code>Manual</code> makes a redirect the answer instead of following it. The errors:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<code>HttpErr(why)</code>: no answer. The address couldn't be reached, the connection or TLS
					failed, the server broke HTTP, or a header had a line break in it.
				</li>
				<li><code>TimedOut</code>: no whole answer in time, counted from connecting to the body's last byte.</li>
				<li>
					<code>TooLarge</code>: the body was over the limit, refused by its <code>content-length</code> or
					as it went past it.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				<strong>Redirects</strong> are followed as a browser's <code>fetch</code> follows them, 20 at most.
				A 303 (or a 301 or 302 answering a POST) becomes a GET without its body, and
				<code>authorization</code> and <code>cookie</code> headers aren't sent on to another origin.
				<strong>TLS</strong> is rustls, checked against the web's certificate authorities.
				<strong>Nothing is decompressed:</strong> if you send <code>accept-encoding</code>, the body is as
				the server sent it.
			</p>

			<Heading id="connections">Connections and proxies</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_partner.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_partner.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Connections are kept alive. When a request finishes with a connection, the next request to the
				same place (scheme, host and port, and the proxy in between) uses it again, so an API you call
				often costs one TCP and TLS handshake, not one per request. An idle connection is kept for 30
				seconds, or one second less than its server says it keeps one (<code>keep-alive: timeout=5</code>),
				with at most 32 idle connections per place; any number can be open at once. If the server closed a
				kept connection meanwhile, the request is made again on a new one, once: when it was never sent,
				or when sending it twice is safe (GET, HEAD, OPTIONS, TRACE, PUT, DELETE, or a request with an
				<code>idempotency-key</code> header), as Go's client does.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>The environment's proxy</strong> (<code>FromEnv</code>, the default), read as Go reads it:
					<code>HTTPS_PROXY</code> for an <code>https</code> URL and <code>HTTP_PROXY</code> for an
					<code>http</code> one. It is never used for <code>localhost</code> or a loopback address, nor for a
					host <code>NO_PROXY</code> names. <code>NO_PROXY</code> takes <code>*</code>, an address, a range
					(<code>10.0.0.0/8</code>), <code>example.com</code> (that name and every name under it), or
					<code>.example.com</code> (only the names under it), each optionally with a <code>:port</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A request's own proxy</strong>: <code>Direct</code> uses none; <code>{"Via(url)"}</code>
					uses that proxy for every host. A proxy's URL is <code>http://</code> or <code>https://</code>; a
					user and password in it are sent as <code>proxy-authorization</code>. A proxy URL that can't be
					read is an <code>HttpErr</code>, never a silent way around the proxy.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Through the proxy</strong>, an <code>http</code> URL's request is sent to the proxy
					whole. An <code>https</code> one goes through a tunnel (<code>CONNECT</code>) with TLS to the
					server inside it, so the proxy sees none of it.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="chunks">Reading an answer as it arrives</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_lines.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_lines.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>{"Http.fetch_chunks!(url, init, start, step!)"}</code> makes the same request as
				<code>fetch!</code>, but never holds the whole answer. It calls <code>step!</code> with
				<code>{"Head(res)"}</code> first (the status and headers, the body empty), then with
				<code>{"Chunk(bytes)"}</code> for each part of the body as it arrives. Like a fold, each call gets
				what <code>step!</code> returned last, starting from <code>start</code>, and what it returns after
				the last chunk is the result. If <code>step!</code> returns an <code>Err</code>, the request ends
				there and its connection closes (<code>{"Err(Stopped(why))"}</code>).
			</p>
			<p>
				Use it for a body too large to hold, or one to pass on as it comes, such as a language model's
				answer, word by word, sent on to a <a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#streams"}>stream of
				events</a>. <code>timeout_ms</code> runs to the body's last byte and <code>max_bytes</code> counts
				every chunk, so for a stream that runs long, set both to 0.
			</p>

			<Heading id="bytes">Request bodies are bytes</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_digest.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_digest.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				A request's <code>body</code> is a <code>List(U8)</code>, never decoded on the way, so an upload is
				exactly what the client sent. <code>Server.text_of(request)</code> reads it as UTF-8 text (with
				U+FFFD where it isn't valid). <code>{"Server.bytes(status, headers, bytes)"}</code> answers with bytes
				as they are.
			</p>
			<p>
				An <a href={"${Paths.routing}#endpoints"}>endpoint</a> decodes its input from the body only when the
				body is JSON (<code>application/json</code>, or a type ending in <code>+json</code>) or a form
				(URL-encoded, or <code>multipart/form-data</code>, whose files come as their names). For a body of
				any other type (an image, a video), give the handler the input type <code>{"{}"}</code> and read
				<code>request.body</code> yourself; a handler that expects fields answers such a body 415. The files a multipart form sent are <code>Server.files(request)</code>, or one by its field
				with <code>Server.file(request, field)</code>, each with its name, type and bytes.
			</p>
			<p>
				A route file may also name the rules its body is read and checked with
				(<code>post_body = Signup.form</code>, a form of <a href={Paths.ecosystem_forms}>packages/forms</a>).
				A form's fields and a JSON object are then read alike, and what the rules refuse never reaches the
				handler: it is answered 422, with the issues as JSON
				(<a href={"${Paths.routing}#endpoints"}>endpoints</a>).
			</p>
			<p>
				A request's body is read whole before your code runs, 16 MiB at most unless the server's
				<code>--max-body</code> (<code>MAX_BODY</code>) allows more
				(<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#running"}>running it</a>).
			</p>

			<Heading id="forward">Passing on a body from elsewhere</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_cdn.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_cdn.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>{"Server.forward(upstream, headers)"}</code> is an answer whose body never passes through
				Roc. The host asks <code>upstream</code> and sends on what comes as it comes, so a 500 MB video
				costs your code nothing and starts playing at once. The answer takes upstream's status (200, 206 for
				a range, 304, 404) and the headers that describe its body (<code>content-type</code>,
				<code>content-length</code>, <code>content-range</code>, <code>accept-ranges</code>,
				<code>etag</code>, <code>last-modified</code>); the headers you give are added, or replace those.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Only what your code says goes upstream.</strong> The host adds nothing from the request,
					so its cookies stay here. <code>Server.passed_on(request)</code> gives the request's
					<code>Range</code>, <code>If-Range</code>, <code>If-None-Match</code> and
					<code>If-Modified-Since</code> headers, to pass on as upstream's. Change the request record first
					(drop a range that doesn't fit, say) and you change what is asked.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>An error upstream</strong> (400 or more) is answered with its status and its standard
					reason as text, never with upstream's body, which might name a bucket. No answer at all is a 502
					(a 504 after 30 s), logged without the URL's query, where a presigned URL keeps its signature. A
					body cut short closes the client's connection, so it is never passed off as complete.
				</li>
				<li>
					The storage package's <code>Storage.serve!</code> is a forward to S3 for an S3 bucket
					(<a href={"${Paths.ecosystem_storage}#serve"}>storage</a>); for a directory or memory, the server
					sends the file it keeps (<code>Server.stored</code>).
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="cookies">Cookies</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_cookies.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_cookies.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>{"Server.set_cookie(answer, name, value, options)"}</code> adds a <code>Set-Cookie</code>
				header. <code>Server.CookieOpts</code> holds its attributes, and <code>Server.cookie_defaults</code>
				the usual ones, which you change with a record update: path <code>/</code>,
				<code>http_only</code>, <code>same_site: Lax</code>, this host alone, until the browser closes, and
				sent over plain HTTP too.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<code>max_age_s</code> (<code>Max-Age</code>) and <code>expires_ms</code> (<code>Expires</code>, a
					moment in milliseconds since the epoch) are left out when 0. With neither, the cookie ends when
					the browser closes.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>secure</code>, <code>http_only</code>, <code>same_site</code> (<code>Lax</code>,
					<code>Strict</code> or <code>None</code>), <code>path</code> and <code>domain</code> are each left
					out when empty. Browsers drop a <code>__Host-</code> cookie unless it is <code>secure</code>, has
					the path <code>/</code> and no domain, and drop <code>SameSite=None</code> without
					<code>secure</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>Server.clear_cookie</code> takes the path, domain and <code>secure</code> the cookie was set
					with. <code>Server.with_cookie</code> and <code>Server.without_cookie</code> set and clear one with
					the defaults. The value is <code>%XX</code>-encoded, and <code>Server.cookie(request, name)</code>
					decodes it.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="routes">.well-known, and every method</Heading>
			<Code
				title="routes/"
				source={".well-known/oauth-authorization-server/+server.roc   # GET /.well-known/oauth-authorization-server\nuploads/[name]/+server.roc                             # put!: an upload, its body the bytes\nmcp/+server.roc                                        # respond!: every method, as it likes"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
				plain={Bool.True}
			/>
			<p>
				A directory named <code>.well-known</code> is routed like any other, so the documents other servers
				look for there (an OAuth server's metadata, <code>security.txt</code>) are ordinary route files. Any
				other directory whose name begins with a dot isn't routed.
			</p>
			<p>
				A <code>+server.roc</code> normally defines one function per method (<code>get!</code>,
				<code>post!</code>, <code>put!</code>…). One that defines <code>respond!</code> also answers every
				method the file doesn't name, given the request, the path's params and the context, and returns a
				<code>Server.Response</code> of its own. Use it for a protocol that speaks over POST and GET at one
				URL, or a page a package draws (<a href={"${Paths.ecosystem_jobs}#page"}>the jobs page</a>). It
				isn't an endpoint, so it stays out of <code>/openapi.json</code>
				(<a href={"${Paths.routing}#endpoints"}>endpoints</a>).
			</p>
			<Code source={"## Every method, as it comes.\nrespond! : { request : Server.Request, params : {}, context : c } => Server.Response\nrespond! = |{ request, .. }| Server.response(200, [{ name: \"content-type\", value: \"text/plain\" }], \"\${request.method} \${Server.text_of(request)}\")"} numbers={Bool.False} />

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>HTTP/1.1 only</strong>, each way: no HTTP/2, and no SOCKS proxy.</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A request's body is whole</strong> in Roc, coming in or going out: it can't be streamed
					as it comes. (An answer from another server can be read in chunks, and a forwarded body never
					enters Roc.)
				</li>
			</ul>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
